WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.087
Mr. Phileas Fogg was a mystery. No one knew how he had made his fortune, but there was no doubt that he was rich.

2
00:00:08.088 --> 00:00:13.109
He was in his forties, tall, well-built, with handsome features.

3
00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:16.997
He had light hair, pale skin, and excellent teeth.

4
00:00:16.998 --> 00:00:23.123
He was one of the most prominent members of London's Reform Club, and yet he attracted little attention.

5
00:00:23.124 --> 00:00:27.661
It was clear to those who knew him that Phileas Fogg was a traveler.

6
00:00:27.662 --> 00:00:33.446
He was familiar with the world and knew the details of even the most remote areas of the earth.

7
00:00:33.447 --> 00:00:39.189
It was also certain, however, that Mr. Fogg had not left London in many years.

8
00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:46.702
He lived a quiet single life with no wife or children to keep him company, and his daily routine never changed.

9
00:00:46.703 --> 00:00:54.578
On October 2, 1872, Mr. Fogg sat squarely in his armchair in his mansion on Savile Row.

10
00:00:54.579 --> 00:00:58.753
His hands were resting on his knees, and his body was straight.

11
00:00:58.754 --> 00:01:07.395
He was watching a complicated clock that told the seconds, minutes, hours, days, months, and years all at once.

12
00:01:07.396 --> 00:01:14.417
A knock sounded at the front door, and before long, Mr. Fogg's servant, who was leaving that day, appeared.

13
00:01:14.418 --> 00:01:19.212
"The new servant is here to see you, sir," he said.

14
00:01:19.213 --> 00:01:23.755
Then a young man about thirty years old entered the room and bowed.

15
00:01:23.756 --> 00:01:29.182
"You are a Frenchman, I believe," asked Phileas Fogg, "and your name is John?"

16
00:01:29.183 --> 00:01:33.382
"Jean, if monsieur pleases," replied the newcomer.

17
00:01:33.383 --> 00:01:41.563
"My name is Jean Passepartout. My surname suits me because I have a natural ability to move from one business to another.

18
00:01:41.564 --> 00:01:44.220
I believe I'm honest, monsieur, and I've had several jobs.

19
00:01:44.221 --> 00:01:51.778
I've been a singer, a circus acrobat, a tight-rope walker, a gymnastics teacher, and finally, a fireman in Paris."

20
00:01:51.779 --> 00:01:57.468
"Well, Mr. Passepartout," said Mr. Fogg, "you have been well recommended to me.

21
00:01:57.469 --> 00:02:00.005
I have heard good reports about you."

22
00:02:00.006 --> 00:02:07.626
"Thank you, monsieur," said Passepartout. "I wish now to enjoy a quiet domestic life as a valet here in England.

23
00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:14.455
I have heard that you are a most exact and settled gentleman, so I have come in the hope of living a tranquil life.

24
00:02:14.456 --> 00:02:17.609
Maybe I will even forget the name of Passepartout."

25
00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.658
"You know my conditions?" asked Mr. Fogg.

26
00:02:20.659 --> 00:02:23.207
"Yes, monsieur," said Passepartout.

27
00:02:23.208 --> 00:02:26.847
"Good! What time is it?" asked Mr. Fogg.

28
00:02:26.848 --> 00:02:33.989
Taking a huge silver watch from his pocket, Passepartout replied, "Twenty-five minutes after eleven."

29
00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:37.506
"Your watch is slow," said Mr. Fogg.

30
00:02:37.507 --> 00:02:41.542
"Pardon me, monsieur, it is impossible," said Passepartout.

31
00:02:41.543 --> 00:02:45.451
"It is four minutes too slow," said Mr. Fogg.

32
00:02:45.452 --> 00:02:49.041
"No matter. It's enough only to mention the error.

33
00:02:49.042 --> 00:02:58.584
Now from this moment, 29 minutes after 11 a.m., this Wednesday, the second of October, you are in my service."

34
00:02:58.585 --> 00:03:03.992
Mr. Fogg stood up, and without a further word, he put on his hat and left.

35
00:03:03.993 --> 00:03:10.785
Every day, at exactly 11:30 a.m., Mr. Fogg left his house and walked to the Reform Club.

36
00:03:10.786 --> 00:03:15.799
He was so precise that he never appeared to be rushed or agitated in any way.

37
00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:22.763
He was the most deliberate person in the world and always reached his destination at the very moment that he was due. At

38
00:03:22.764 --> 00:03:28.927
half-past eleven, then, Passepartout found himself alone in the house on Savile Row. He

39
00:03:28.928 --> 00:03:33.960
began an inspection and was pleased to find the house clean and arranged neatly. When

40
00:03:33.961 --> 00:03:41.017
Passepartout reached the second floor, he recognized at once the room that was meant for him. Hanging

41
00:03:41.018 --> 00:03:46.587
over the clock above the fireplace was the daily routine of the house, written on a card. It

42
00:03:46.588 --> 00:03:54.507
explained Passepartout's duties, from eight in the morning, when Mr. Fogg got up, until half-past eleven, when Mr. Fogg left for his club. It

43
00:03:54.508 --> 00:04:03.885
explained the tea and toast at 8:23, the shaving water at 9:37, and the washcloth, soap, and washbowl at 9:40. In

44
00:04:03.886 --> 00:04:09.026
Mr. Fogg's closet each pair of pants, coat, and vest had a number on it. This

45
00:04:09.027 --> 00:04:13.188
indicated the time of year and the season when it would be worn.Mr. Fogg

46
00:04:13.189 --> 00:04:17.345
used the same system for his shoes as well. Having inspected

47
00:04:17.346 --> 00:04:22.871
the house from top to bottom, Passepartout rubbed his hands together. "This is

48
00:04:22.872 --> 00:04:26.165
just what I wanted!" he said. "We will

49
00:04:26.166 --> 00:04:29.835
get along well together, Mr. Fogg and I!" Meanwhile Mr.

50
00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:36.206
Phileas Fogg had put his right foot before his left foot 575 times and his

51
00:04:36.207 --> 00:04:42.804
left foot before his right foot 576 times and reached the Reform Club. He immediately

52
00:04:42.805 --> 00:04:49.766
went into the dining room and took his place at his usual table, which had already been set for him. Phileas Fogg's

53
00:04:49.767 --> 00:04:56.361
lunch consisted of a broiled fish with sauce and a slice of roast beef garnished with mushrooms. He also

54
00:04:56.362 --> 00:05:02.084
ate a rhubarb and gooseberry tart and a morsel of Cheshire cheese. The entire

55
00:05:02.085 --> 00:05:06.090
meal was washed down with several cups of tea. Mr. Fogg

56
00:05:06.091 --> 00:05:10.960
rose at thirteen minutes to one and went into the large hall. He found

57
00:05:10.961 --> 00:05:18.689
his regular chair in his regular corner and happily sat down to pass the afternoon as he did every day: quietly reading

58
00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:26.797
the newspapers amidst the comfortable and lavish surroundings of his club. Passepartout's Guidebook

59
00:05:26.798 --> 00:05:33.272
Hello. I am Jean Passepartout, and the year is 1872, during the Victorian Era.

60
00:05:33.273 --> 00:05:37.332
London is the largest and most modern city on earth.

61
00:05:37.333 --> 00:05:41.522
Its streets are very crowded with people and horse-drawn carriages.

62
00:05:41.523 --> 00:05:48.217
Below the streets, trains, which were invented less than fifty years ago, travel through the London Underground.

63
00:05:48.218 --> 00:05:50.935
A lot of great scientific discovery has occurred lately.

64
00:05:50.936 --> 00:05:55.497
At night London's streets are now lit by gas lamps.

65
00:05:55.498 --> 00:06:00.889
If you have an urgent message to send, you can send it by electronic wire, using Morse code.

66
00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:08.103
Perhaps you've heard of Morse code? It uses a series of dots and dashes to relay information.

67
00:06:08.104 --> 00:06:13.407
London is the capital not only of England but also of the vast British Empire.

68
00:06:13.408 --> 00:06:17.491
Queen Victoria rules over almost one-quarter of the earth's land.

69
00:06:17.492 --> 00:06:21.899
People often say, "The sun never sets on the British Empire."

70
00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:29.453
That's because it's always daytime in at least one of the Queen's territories.

